====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. Role classification works as predicted.
   - Forward chain: 3 classes — head / middles / tail. Middle positions
     correctly collapse.
   - Out-star / in-star: 2 classes — pivot vs. leaves. Direction of the
     star is reflected in which slot the pivot occupies.
   - 3-cycle: 1 class. Rotational symmetry captured — consistent with
     the graph's actual automorphism structure.

2. Bidirectional chain collapses endpoints AND middles into a unified
   "Both" structure.
   - a1 and a4 share (out=1, in=1, Both) — the profile correctly sees
     them as symmetric endpoints of a bidirectional structure, not as
     a one-way "head" and "tail."
   - a2 and a3 share (out=2, in=2, Both) — they are the "denser" middles.
   - This is the first case where `SlotPattern::Both` hides meaningful
     internal variation. `out=1 in=1 Both` covers both "bidirectional
     endpoint" and "one-way middle" — they are distinguishable by degree
     alone here, but more complex graphs could blur the line.

3. Disjoint unions merge equivalent roles across components.
   - Two copies of a 3-chain produce exactly 3 classes (not 6). p1/q1
     share head-role; p2/q2 share middle-role; p3/q3 share tail-role.
   - Useful property for later pattern detection: "similar structures in
     different places" are recognized as similar without extra machinery.

====================================================================
WHAT THIS ENABLES / WHAT IT DOES NOT
====================================================================

Enables:
  - "These identifiers play the same role" — role-level clustering.
  - Role-based filtering: later mechanisms can pick the representative
    of each class instead of iterating every identifier.
  - Cross-component recognition of similar structures.

Does NOT enable:
  - Naming a compound pattern. We see that p1, p2, p3 belong to classes
    {LeftOnly-1, Both-1-1, RightOnly-1} respectively, but we have no
    mechanism that says "there is a chain-of-3 pattern spanning these."
  - Distinguishing "the second middle" from "the third middle" in a
    long chain. Positions within a class collapse.
  - Pattern naming (the design-notes target). That requires compound
    machinery above this layer.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0004's 0-hop signature is sufficient for the role-classification
layer. No immediate need to upgrade to 1-hop.

The next layer up — pattern detection / naming — is what's needed
before the "construct integer type" target from the design notes becomes
reachable. Questions to resolve before building it:

  (a) Does a "pattern" live over R instances, or over identifiers?
      (An edge (x -> y) where x has profile P1 and y has profile P2 is
      one kind; a connected subgraph is another. Design-notes leans
      toward subgraphs; the former is cheaper.)
  (b) How is a pattern named? Per commitment 3, types are meta-R
      instances. So a pattern name is itself an identifier in the same
      R space, linked to its instances by new R instances.
  (c) What triggers the system to *try* naming a pattern? (Self-drive.)

These are ADR 0005+ material. Not yet committed to a direction.
